1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Nature may abhor a vacuum, but we seem to love them.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
They clean our floors and deliver medicine.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
They've got the suction power needed by industry.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
They're first on the scene at catastrophic sites.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
And they battle algae that's destroying coral reefs.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:20,000
They even cut our hair.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
From little suckers to industrial giants.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Vacuums on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Ground Zero, 9-11.

10
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,060
Hurricane Katrina.

11
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:55,300
Plane crashes, train wrecks, catastrophes on a monstrous scale.

12
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,800
When calamity strikes, who cleans up?

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,160
Tradewind's environmental restoration in Holtzville, New York,

14
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:06,980
has become the company to call for mop-up after disaster.

15
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:10,300
The single most important tool of their trade?

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:11,700
Vacuums.

17
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:17,740
I can't imagine a business that uses vacuums more than we do.

18
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:22,340
It would be very difficult for us to do any kind of cleaning without vacuums.

19
00:01:22,341 --> 00:01:24,560
And that's almost across the board,

20
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,220
whether it's hazardous waste or controlled waste

21
00:01:28,221 --> 00:01:30,820
or even just after fires and floods.

22
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,700
After the collapse of the World Trade Center towers,

23
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,300
Tradewinds was on site from day one.

24
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,640
At Ground Zero, we needed vacuums.

25
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:43,740
The reason why we needed the vacuums

26
00:01:43,741 --> 00:01:46,620
was due to the fact that we had 210 stories that collapsed.

27
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:48,980
We had drywall dust.

28
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:49,860
We had cement dust.

29
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:50,680
We had asbestos.

30
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:52,020
We had insulation.

31
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:55,060
It all made up of dust that we never had before

32
00:01:55,061 --> 00:01:56,560
on a disaster site.

33
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:00,980
The fallout was up to your kneecaps and some of the buildings.

34
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:06,960
Tradewinds employed over 2,000 workers and hundreds of heavy-duty vacuums

35
00:02:06,961 --> 00:02:11,100
to clean the toxic dust from over 4 million square feet of building space,

36
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:14,380
the equivalent of 70 football fields.

37
00:02:14,381 --> 00:02:23,420
We used a multitude of vacuums from very small heifer vacuums with special filtration

38
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:30,280
to trailer-mounted vacuums, where we actually put whole segments of buildings under a vacuum,

39
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:35,680
which actually constrained any contaminants that might be in these buildings from getting out.

40
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:43,280
Hurricane Katrina's 22 million tons of waterlogged debris presented a different challenge.

41
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:49,820
Once the water receded, then we had to bring in the wet vacs to start cleaning up the biohazardous materials.

42
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:54,020
The water was so high that you had a lot of sewage, raw sewage.

43
00:02:54,620 --> 00:03:05,160
And once everything dried out, you had probably about a five or six foot layer in some places of a bio of sewage that we had to clean up with wetbacks.

44
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:10,260
Liquid, powder, slurry, or sludge.

45
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:13,840
Tradewind's vacuum sucked up everything Katrina threw at them.

46
00:03:13,841 --> 00:03:19,420
We mobilized the same day Katrina was unfortunately hitting Louisiana,

47
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:25,040
and we responded with some large caravans of specialized equipment,

48
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,440
lots of vacuums, and lots of people.

49
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:31,520
How was Tradewinds able to respond so quickly?

50
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:36,400
When there's a large catastrophe, where do you go to get what you need?

51
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:37,880
You better have it.

52
00:03:37,881 --> 00:03:46,500
In our warehouse, which is 70,000 square feet on seven acres of land,

53
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:52,560
we have over 100 specialized vehicles as well as just about anything you can conceive

54
00:03:52,561 --> 00:04:00,240
that you would need to do cleanups, including 20-plus varieties of vacuums,

55
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:02,800
from the smallest to the largest.

56
00:04:02,801 --> 00:04:08,040
One of the largest mobile systems the Tradewinds used for both 9-11 and Katrina

57
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:11,320
is the Guzzler, a giant that lives up to its name,

58
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:14,300
with 200 times the suction of a typical home vacuum.

59
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:21,220
We use this on emergency oil spills, mostly for solids, but we can also use it for liquids.

60
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,400
It has a capacity of 18 cubic yards of debris,

61
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:30,500
and it has a suction of around 5,000 cubic foot a minute.

62
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,480
That's a lot of suction.

63
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,640
These vacuums are incredibly powerful.

64
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:41,280
If the person handling the hose or somebody in close proximity

65
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:45,380
actually had their arm or leg sucked up into the vacuum,

66
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:50,020
you could actually suck the blood right out of a limb.

67
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,020
The Guzzler is specially designed for efficient dumping

68
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:54,320
of its caustic cargo.

69
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,260
This isn't your home vacuum

70
00:05:00,261 --> 00:05:06,760
vacuum and a way that we dump this is made to come over a roll-off container

71
00:05:06,761 --> 00:05:10,880
and actually dump right into the roll-off container and then that container goes

72
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:17,060
into a disposal facility. All of Tradewind's vacuums owe their operation to

73
00:05:17,061 --> 00:05:23,380
the same principle of physics. An impeller such as a fan or pump creates a region

74
00:05:23,381 --> 00:05:28,060
of low pressure or partial vacuum in an interior space. The difference in

75
00:05:28,061 --> 00:05:31,100
between the interior and the exterior spaces

76
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,740
results in the propulsion of matter

77
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:35,160
towards the low pressure area.

78
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:38,000
Narrowing the corridor through which the material passes

79
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:42,380
results in greater suction and greater velocity.

80
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:45,740
When the material being suctioned is a deadly biohazard,

81
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:48,840
special care must be taken to encounter and contain it,

82
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:51,780
as this simulation demonstrates.

83
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:52,920
When you're cleaning up anthrax,

84
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,320
anthrax is spores.

85
00:05:54,321 --> 00:05:59,320
So this vacuum has a HEPA filtration inside.

86
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:08,320
This is a HEPA filter, and this would catch any anthrax spores that were to come through the vacuum

87
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:11,320
and would not leave the vacuum once it's inside.

88
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:20,320
HEPA filters, composed of densely packed layers of glass fiber, remove 99.97% of all airborne particles

89
00:06:20,321 --> 00:06:23,320
that are 0.3 microns or greater in diameter.

90
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:27,920
Toxic mercury spills call for a vacuum with a different filtration system.

91
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:33,560
This vacuum's special in a way that when you suck up the mercury,

92
00:06:33,561 --> 00:06:36,500
it actually comes into the vacuum, drops into a container

93
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:40,660
that we're later able to unscrew and dispose of.

94
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:46,000
This is the media we use to help catch the mercury vapor

95
00:06:46,001 --> 00:06:50,240
that is sucked into the vacuum and it's not released back into the atmosphere

96
00:06:50,241 --> 00:06:52,080
because of these charcoal pellets.

97
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:54,860
In addition to toxic spills,

98
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:58,080
Tradewinds also specializes in dealing with accidents

99
00:06:58,081 --> 00:07:00,820
involving blood-borne pathogens and body parts.

100
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:05,240
One of the most unpleasant cleanups that we have to deal with

101
00:07:05,241 --> 00:07:10,160
are suicides or major crime scenes.

102
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:13,880
And again, we use special equipment and a lot of vacuums

103
00:07:13,881 --> 00:07:16,060
to address these type of cleanups,

104
00:07:16,061 --> 00:07:20,920
and it's one of the more difficult arenas of what we do.

105
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:25,020
One of the most unusual uses of vacuum technology

106
00:07:25,021 --> 00:07:26,100
that Tradewinds employs

107
00:07:26,101 --> 00:07:28,760
is for the recovery of water-damaged papers.

108
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:31,960
This is our thermal vacuum freeze dryer

109
00:07:31,961 --> 00:07:35,180
used to restore documents, books,

110
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:37,900
that have been wetted or flooded out.

111
00:07:37,901 --> 00:07:40,520
The machine uses a sublimation process

112
00:07:40,521 --> 00:07:45,620
which freezes the books to about a negative 50 degrees Celsius,

113
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:49,360
and you create a vacuum inside the chamber,

114
00:07:49,361 --> 00:07:52,620
and the ice turns to a gas phase,

115
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:54,900
the gas transfers over to a condenser

116
00:07:54,901 --> 00:07:57,440
and then back to ice again on the condenser

117
00:07:57,441 --> 00:08:01,260
so that the books or documents never go through a wet phase again.

118
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:05,740
A document would normally stay in here for one or two weeks.

119
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:09,240
When these documents come out, they'll be totally dry.

120
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:14,040
The same powerful vacuums used for emergency response work

121
00:08:14,041 --> 00:08:17,140
can also be found sucking rocks on construction sites.

122
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:21,560
What these contractors are doing is they're using a hurricane vacuum

123
00:08:21,561 --> 00:08:24,100
to remove the rock that is on this roof deck.

124
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:30,240
The reason that rock is down here is to hold the roof down in wind.

125
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:36,100
Before the roof can be replaced or repaired, this rock has to be removed.

126
00:08:37,380 --> 00:08:41,420
To manually remove these stones from an area this large would take days.

127
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:44,700
A vacuum can do the job in a fraction of that time,

128
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:46,880
while eliminating the back-breaking labor.

129
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:50,340
This rock that they're removing today is quite heavy.

130
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:54,040
It takes a very powerful vacuum to move it the distances they are.

131
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:57,400
You can imagine trying to pick this up with your shop vacuum at home.

132
00:08:57,460 --> 00:08:58,820
It probably wouldn't even move it.

133
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:05,520
The contractors rely on the hurricane vacuum to suction these stones through tubes to their truck below.

134
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:10,640
The hurricane vacuums, the way they work is the material is pulled through the line,

135
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:17,000
and it enters the vacuum and goes into a cyclone separator where the material spins and then drops into the collection cone.

136
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:21,720
The air and the dust that pulls this material in there go up through the filter bags,

137
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:24,260
which are in the top of the cyclone separator.

138
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:27,000
That air gets filtered out through 37 filter bags,

139
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:30,260
and then the air passes down through another set of filters,

140
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:33,300
and then through the vacuum pump, and then out to atmosphere.

141
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:40,540
Large vacuums like the Hurricane can move up to 4,500 cubic feet of air per minute.

142
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:45,480
This is the diesel engine on a Hurricane vacuum.

143
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:47,900
This is a 250 horsepower.

144
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,040
We build from 170 to 500 horsepower.

145
00:09:51,420 --> 00:09:56,240
It's a turbocharged diesel, fuel-injected, tier 2 engine.

146
00:09:57,340 --> 00:10:03,140
A large engine can generate enough airflow to lift a 4-pound stone 100 feet vertically.

147
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:07,000
That's 115 times the lift of a typical home vacuum.

148
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:12,720
The farther out a contractor runs his vacuum line,

149
00:10:12,721 --> 00:10:15,680
will reduce the suction at the end of the line.

150
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:19,100
The vacuums are very efficient, up to about 300, 400 feet.

151
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:22,220
If they go out farther than that, it will still pull the material.

152
00:10:22,340 --> 00:10:23,660
It's just going to be a little bit slower.

153
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:29,160
Big suckers like these are manufactured in Exonia, Wisconsin.

154
00:10:29,980 --> 00:10:32,880
When building these vacuums, we start with raw product.

155
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:35,900
We build the trailer frame or the skid, depending on which it is.

156
00:10:35,901 --> 00:10:38,920
They sit on the motor or the diesel engine,

157
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:42,240
set the blowers on, the bag house.

158
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:47,060
And then they get into the electrical, the hydraulics,

159
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:49,300
and after that, they put the wheels on,

160
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:51,880
take it to paint, and it's a hurricane vacuum.

161
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:58,300
A 70-pound I-beam doesn't pose a challenge for the hurricane.

162
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:01,260
A 16-pound bowling ball?

163
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:02,460
No sweat.

164
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,680
In fact, the hurricane could lift 24 of them at once.

165
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:08,900
To show the real strength of this vacuum,

166
00:11:08,901 --> 00:11:12,020
we're going to implode a 55-gallon steel drum.

167
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:23,560
Want to see it again?

168
00:11:23,561 --> 00:11:29,180
As industrial vacuums get bigger and bigger, the demand for their powerful suction grows

169
00:11:29,181 --> 00:11:30,900
as well.

170
00:11:30,901 --> 00:11:35,240
Industry continues to find new uses for these vacuum giants.

171
00:11:35,241 --> 00:11:40,780
And while they may be able to inhale mountains of material, the humble household vacuum has

172
00:11:40,781 --> 00:11:46,080
found a way to do battle with millions of microscopic monsters lurking beneath our feet.

173
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:54,060
Airborne particles that can lodge in your lungs, pesticide residue, and deadly toxins

174
00:11:54,061 --> 00:11:57,742
waiting to be inhaled. Viruses, germs, dust mites.

175
00:11:57,743 --> 00:12:00,320
If you believe the vacuum appliance

176
00:12:00,321 --> 00:12:04,500
industry, household dirt has never been more threatening.

177
00:12:04,501 --> 00:12:07,140
America's obsession with cleanliness

178
00:12:07,141 --> 00:12:09,760
has sent sales for new vacuums skyrocketing,

179
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:14,460
especially those that feature powerful suction and filtration systems.

180
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:21,180
It's a business that generated $2.7 billion in U.S. sales in 2006.

181
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,700
Tektronic Industries in Glenwillow, Ohio,

182
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:28,200
which recently acquired Hoover FloorCare,

183
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:32,780
does research at its testing facility to improve and refine the household vacuum.

184
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:36,080
Keita, the good vacuum cleaner is all about performance.

185
00:12:36,081 --> 00:12:40,860
Our goal is to achieve 300 to 500 hours of life on these cleaners.

186
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:45,400
That equates to roughly 10 to 15 years of life.

187
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:49,540
In addition to rigorous testing for product durability,

188
00:12:50,380 --> 00:12:53,740
TTI also tests for leaks in the vacuum that could reduce suction.

189
00:12:55,880 --> 00:13:01,440
Thermal tests assure that vacuums don't generate heat past UL limits to avoid fire hazards.

190
00:13:01,441 --> 00:13:05,840
They even perform audio tests.

191
00:13:07,300 --> 00:13:11,340
Six strategically placed microphones pick up different frequencies of the vacuum's sound.

192
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:17,740
Annoying high-pitched tones can be attenuated to improve sound quality.

193
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:21,600
The vacuum also has to appeal to the customer's eye.

194
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:30,440
I think over the last several years, the trends have been toward more colorful, more engaging products.

195
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:33,980
that give you that positive reinforcement

196
00:13:33,981 --> 00:13:36,120
that I'm getting the work done.

197
00:13:36,121 --> 00:13:39,320
The clear plastic housings used in bagless vacuums

198
00:13:39,321 --> 00:13:42,940
allow consumers to see their performance.

199
00:13:42,941 --> 00:13:45,240
Bagless, or cyclonic vacuums, are

200
00:13:45,241 --> 00:13:47,260
like smaller versions of the hurricane,

201
00:13:47,261 --> 00:13:50,160
creating a vortex of air that forces heavier dirt particles

202
00:13:50,161 --> 00:13:53,580
to the outside wall of the suction column.

203
00:13:53,581 --> 00:13:56,580
The weight of the dirt causes it to fall into the container,

204
00:13:56,581 --> 00:14:00,460
where it can be collected for later disposal.

205
00:14:00,461 --> 00:14:02,620
New designs are driving the home vacuum market,

206
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:05,940
including the cutting-edge look of Dirt Devil's cone.

207
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:12,240
The cone looks like a decorative device

208
00:14:12,241 --> 00:14:13,520
that you might just see on a table.

209
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:15,380
But at the end of the day, you can go over

210
00:14:15,381 --> 00:14:16,880
and you can pick up the top of the cone

211
00:14:16,881 --> 00:14:17,860
and you have a hand back.

212
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:21,280
Of course, a vacuum is only as good as the person using it.

213
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:25,120
And if you want to rule out human error in your housekeeping,

214
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:27,160
check out the Roomba,

215
00:14:27,161 --> 00:14:31,660
The robotic vacuum that sweeps floors autonomously,

216
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:35,200
using sensors to calculate the size and shape of a room

217
00:14:35,201 --> 00:14:38,800
as it vacuums around hard-to-reach areas, like under furniture.

218
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:42,420
Worried about germs and dust mites in your carpet?

219
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:46,520
Try out the Halo UVX that employs ultraviolet light

220
00:14:46,521 --> 00:14:48,920
to destroy germs and viruses as it sweeps.

221
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:52,700
Don't want to lug around a heavy appliance?

222
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:55,400
Just plug into a central vacuum system.

223
00:14:55,401 --> 00:15:02,360
Tubes embedded in the walls of a home or business suction dirt to a central out-of-the-way collection receptacle.

224
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:06,720
When you're done with all that vacuuming, how about a haircut?

225
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:08,100
If you dare.

226
00:15:08,980 --> 00:15:15,700
The FloBi haircutting system uses the suction of the vacuum to draw hair into razors that trim to preset lengths.

227
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:24,160
This multi-billion dollar floor care business can thank a janitor for its creation.

228
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:30,160
an Ohio custodian who in 1907 was dealing with a health problem.

229
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:37,020
James Murray Spangler was a janitor at a local department store who had asthma,

230
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:42,360
and out of a need to alleviate his discomfort, he had put together an electric suction sweeper.

231
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:47,700
Using a sewing machine motor, a fan, a broom handle, and a pillowcase,

232
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,600
Spangler constructed a device that revolutionized housekeeping.

233
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:54,700
The first successful portable electric vacuum.

234
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:58,960
Using it himself at work, he thought it was a success,

235
00:15:58,961 --> 00:16:02,480
so he applied for a patent in 1907.

236
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:06,140
He received that patent in June of 1908.

237
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:09,880
W.H. Hoover, a successful local harness

238
00:16:09,881 --> 00:16:11,280
and leather goods manufacturer,

239
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:13,720
foresaw his business fortunes dwindling

240
00:16:13,721 --> 00:16:15,460
with the advent of the horseless carriage.

241
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,340
Recognizing Spangler's invention as an opportunity,

242
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:23,960
Hoover obtained the rights to manufacture it on a grand scale.

243
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,900
In 1908, the Hoover Company released its first vacuum.

244
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:32,920
But at the time, only 10% of America had electricity,

245
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:36,120
and that electricity was used to power electric lights.

246
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:40,620
The absence of home wall outlets required the Hoover power cord

247
00:16:40,621 --> 00:16:42,220
to screw into a light bulb socket.

248
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:48,420
This is one of Hoover's first vacuum cleaners, the Model O, from 1908.

249
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:50,300
This is a well-used Model O.

250
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:52,100
It weighed 40 pounds.

251
00:16:52,580 --> 00:16:56,000
25 pounds of that was due to the weight of the motor itself.

252
00:16:57,600 --> 00:17:02,700
The fan-powered Model O is one of the rarest items at the Hoover Historical Center,

253
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:08,340
a vacuum cleaner museum that operates out of the former Hoover family home in North Canton, Ohio.

254
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:12,580
Exhibits here follow the evolution of the vacuum cleaner over the decades.

255
00:17:13,580 --> 00:17:16,760
This is Hoover's Model 700 from 1926.

256
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:21,160
This introduced the use of the beater bar on the agitator.

257
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:24,560
The beater bar brushed the carpet to raise dust

258
00:17:24,561 --> 00:17:26,060
that was suctioned into the vacuum

259
00:17:26,061 --> 00:17:28,060
and gave rise to Hoover's slogan,

260
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:30,700
it beats as it sweeps as it cleans.

261
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:34,660
This is the first Hoover handheld vacuum cleaner

262
00:17:34,661 --> 00:17:36,500
they introduced in 1930.

263
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:37,920
It's called a Model 100

264
00:17:37,921 --> 00:17:43,280
and was available with a price tag of $16.50.

265
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:47,500
Today, you can look at a vacuum cleaner

266
00:17:47,501 --> 00:17:49,120
and basically by the color,

267
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:52,020
it can tell you what era it would have been made in.

268
00:17:52,100 --> 00:18:02,570
It was due to the use of plastics and their technology that they could make the vacuum cleaners more lightweight and making them aesthetically more attractive with

269
00:18:02,571 --> 00:18:03,271
colors.

270
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:07,140
If you really find vacuums attractive, you might try collecting them.

271
00:18:07,740 --> 00:18:11,740
There is a vacuum cleaner collector's club with membership nationally.

272
00:18:11,741 --> 00:18:16,420
A member of the Vacuum Cleaner Collectors Club, who owns one of the largest and most

273
00:18:16,421 --> 00:18:22,820
valuable private collections, is a 12-year-old Michigan boy who's something of a vacuum savant.

274
00:18:22,821 --> 00:18:28,780
This is my vacuum cleaner collection, yet this is not all of my vacuum cleaners.

275
00:18:28,781 --> 00:18:34,180
I actually have so many vacuum cleaners that they spill out to different parts of my house.

276
00:18:34,181 --> 00:18:37,680
This is my bedroom.

277
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:43,020
And as you can see, it is very full with vacuum cleaners, very crowded.

278
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:50,480
My very favorite vacuum cleaner in my entire collection is my vintage Kenmore Magichord from 1978.

279
00:18:50,481 --> 00:19:00,060
It is a two-speed motor with twin fans and a speed selector, and definitely my very favorite because it was one of Sears Kenmore's best.

280
00:19:00,061 --> 00:19:06,060
Another very unique vacuum cleaner is my 1911 pump vacuum,

281
00:19:06,061 --> 00:19:11,060
and it is very unique because you have to pump it when you vacuum.

282
00:19:11,061 --> 00:19:15,060
You really get a good arm exercise with this one.

283
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:23,060
This is my basement where I keep most of my 165 vacuum cleaners.

284
00:19:23,061 --> 00:19:27,060
As you can see, I have mostly old,

285
00:19:27,061 --> 00:19:29,760
and then I have some new vacuum cleaners down here.

286
00:19:29,860 --> 00:19:32,960
I also have some very rare vacuums,

287
00:19:33,060 --> 00:19:38,900
including my 1908 Hoover Model O.

288
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,700
Now, this is the first Hoover vacuum cleaner ever made.

289
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:45,720
Did he say Hoover Model O?

290
00:19:45,820 --> 00:19:47,720
Isn't that the same rarity that's the prize

291
00:19:47,820 --> 00:19:50,420
of the Hoover Historical Center?

292
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:54,720
There is one in the US otherwise, but it's in a museum.

293
00:19:54,820 --> 00:19:56,560
This one I actually own, and I got it

294
00:19:56,561 --> 00:20:02,000
got it for only a dollar, and it's worth over $10,000.

295
00:20:02,001 --> 00:20:04,980
Kyle Kritschbaum's not only a savvy collector,

296
00:20:04,981 --> 00:20:07,200
he repairs and restores antique vacuums

297
00:20:07,201 --> 00:20:08,940
to help fund his hobby.

298
00:20:08,941 --> 00:20:12,340
It's a fascination his father has difficulty explaining.

299
00:20:12,341 --> 00:20:16,620
Kyle's been involved with vacuums for a,

300
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:19,640
probably since infancy.

301
00:20:19,641 --> 00:20:22,800
By the time he was walking, he'd be pushing the vacuum cleaner

302
00:20:22,801 --> 00:20:24,940
around the house all day.

303
00:20:24,941 --> 00:20:29,640
For Halloween one year, we asked him what he wanted to be, and he wanted to be a vacuum.

304
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:36,120
Thanks to Kyle, the Critchpoms have one of the cleanest floors in their neighborhood.

305
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:39,340
He vacuums a couple times a day.

306
00:20:39,740 --> 00:20:43,740
During the summer, he'll do it probably three or four times a day using various vacuums.

307
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:47,840
Kyle hopes one day to turn his hobby into a profession.

308
00:20:47,841 --> 00:20:56,140
I really want to repair vacuum cleaners and have my own shop and sell vacuum cleaners.

309
00:20:56,320 --> 00:21:04,280
I would really also like to invent something, something new, something quiet for a change, a silent vacuum cleaner.

310
00:21:05,580 --> 00:21:08,040
You can be sure that Kyle knows this.

311
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:11,420
Vacuum technology didn't start with the Hoover Company.

312
00:21:12,420 --> 00:21:21,440
Beginning in the 1850s, vacuums began to shrink time and space with elaborate pneumatic delivery systems that once stretched across entire cities.

313
00:21:22,940 --> 00:21:27,920
Today, they move blood samples and medicine across vast hospitals, saving lives.

314
00:21:31,940 --> 00:21:36,740
In busy big city hospitals, efficiency saves lives.

315
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:41,000
By sharing facilities like laboratories and operating rooms,

316
00:21:41,660 --> 00:21:44,680
two adjacent but independent hospitals in Orange, California,

317
00:21:45,160 --> 00:21:48,100
have discovered a way to streamline and improve patient care.

318
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:52,520
And it all hinges on the vacuum.

319
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:58,620
In fact, an elaborate system of six-inch pneumatic vacuum tubes,

320
00:21:59,040 --> 00:22:01,560
thousands of feet long, connects the two hospitals.

321
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:05,700
You have pharmaceuticals, you have medicine, you have physical items,

322
00:22:05,701 --> 00:22:10,040
You have lab specimens that have to get from one point of the hospital to the other point,

323
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:11,360
perhaps a large distance.

324
00:22:11,880 --> 00:22:14,800
And the only way you're going to cover that distance in an optimized way

325
00:22:14,801 --> 00:22:18,840
in terms of cost and taking care of the patients is with a pneumatic air system.

326
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:24,860
On a normal day, the hospital's pneumatic system conducts 200 to 300 transactions an hour.

327
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:30,720
The average transport weighs between 5 and 10 pounds and can travel up to 2,000 feet.

328
00:22:30,721 --> 00:22:36,800
This is a typical tube station.

329
00:22:36,801 --> 00:22:40,880
From here you send and receive items that you want delivered.

330
00:22:40,881 --> 00:22:45,340
You typically take an empty carrier, this is a six inch system.

331
00:22:45,341 --> 00:22:48,280
These are friction bands which just help it along in its travel.

332
00:22:48,281 --> 00:22:51,680
These are the buckles that secure it along its route.

333
00:22:51,681 --> 00:23:00,520
Take your item, place it in there, make sure it's secure, put it in the dispatcher, address

334
00:23:00,521 --> 00:23:04,303
it to where you want it to go, but enter.

335
00:23:04,304 --> 00:23:09,100
Then based on traffic and everything else like that,

336
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:11,270
it'll send when it's clear.

337
00:23:11,271 --> 00:23:16,380
An air stream from a central pump pulls the canister into a series

338
00:23:16,381 --> 00:23:23,660
of galvanized tubes. Programmed transfer units en route direct the canister's path,

339
00:23:23,661 --> 00:23:25,460
diverting it to its proper course.

340
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:31,580
Air pressures of only 3.5 pounds per square inch

341
00:23:31,581 --> 00:23:34,760
push or pull the canister at 16 miles per hour

342
00:23:34,761 --> 00:23:36,340
to its final destination.

343
00:23:38,660 --> 00:23:42,520
We're located in the mechanical room in the basement of the hospital.

344
00:23:42,900 --> 00:23:44,960
This is where the blower motor is,

345
00:23:45,300 --> 00:23:47,400
which is the start of the pneumatic tube system.

346
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:50,580
This motor blows air through the system,

347
00:23:50,581 --> 00:23:54,920
creating pressure to send carriers along.

348
00:23:54,921 --> 00:23:58,780
Three tubes connect to the pump to create propulsion.

349
00:23:58,781 --> 00:24:01,820
To push a canister, the pump blows air into the pressure

350
00:24:01,821 --> 00:24:05,620
port and moves air through the pipes.

351
00:24:05,621 --> 00:24:08,780
To pull a canister, the pressure port closes

352
00:24:08,781 --> 00:24:11,140
and the vacuum port opens.

353
00:24:11,141 --> 00:24:13,580
The pump now suctions air through the vacuum port

354
00:24:13,581 --> 00:24:15,380
and expels it through the exhaust port.

355
00:24:15,381 --> 00:24:23,940
Though this hospital's pneumatic delivery system is state of the art, monitored and

356
00:24:23,941 --> 00:24:27,580
controlled by computers, its basic mechanics are nothing new.

357
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:32,320
What's interesting about the pneumatic tubes is that we are, we're really using the 19th

358
00:24:32,321 --> 00:24:35,540
century standard vacuum blower type technology.

359
00:24:36,540 --> 00:24:41,020
The 1850s saw the first successful application of pneumatic transport.

360
00:24:41,021 --> 00:24:45,740
It was used to transport letters in small tubes.

361
00:24:45,741 --> 00:24:49,700
The first company that used it was a telegraph company in England.

362
00:24:49,701 --> 00:24:53,080
The first application they built was about three blocks long and it went to the London

363
00:24:53,081 --> 00:24:54,680
Stock Exchange.

364
00:24:54,681 --> 00:24:58,460
The Stock Exchange was a major customer sending a lot of messages to and from the telegraph

365
00:24:58,461 --> 00:24:59,580
office.

366
00:24:59,581 --> 00:25:05,180
This allowed them to transport those messages immediately without waiting for a runner boy

367
00:25:05,181 --> 00:25:08,260
to bring them back and forth.

368
00:25:08,261 --> 00:25:12,980
pneumatic system was so successful that it grew to a network of over 30 miles of tubes.

369
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:19,620
An even bigger system was built in Paris where they had ultimately more than 200 miles of tubes

370
00:25:19,621 --> 00:25:25,168
called PMEU and that system was used into the 1980s in Paris.

371
00:25:25,169 --> 00:25:28,260
Being able to move messages within

372
00:25:28,261 --> 00:25:32,260
congested cities at high speeds was a revolutionary concept.

373
00:25:32,261 --> 00:25:35,060
In the 1890s the United States Post Office

374
00:25:35,061 --> 00:25:39,460
decided to try using pneumatic tubes to move mail within cities.

375
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:42,940
So the post office in New York built a system of tubes

376
00:25:42,941 --> 00:25:45,200
that covered the lower half of Manhattan.

377
00:25:46,060 --> 00:25:49,260
The system was used to send letters just between the post offices.

378
00:25:50,260 --> 00:25:52,320
They used tubes that were 8 inches in diameter,

379
00:25:52,321 --> 00:25:56,960
and each carrier was capable of holding 500 letters.

380
00:25:57,520 --> 00:26:00,520
The system delivered mail until 1953,

381
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:03,040
when cheaper truck transport took over.

382
00:26:05,060 --> 00:26:10,060
Pneumatic systems also found broad application in commercial enterprise.

383
00:26:10,061 --> 00:26:14,060
In 1880, Wanamaker's department store in Philadelphia

384
00:26:14,061 --> 00:26:20,060
became the first to install pneumatic tubes to transport documents and money.

385
00:26:20,061 --> 00:26:27,060
The thwunk of pneumatic suction soon became a familiar sound in stores across America.

386
00:26:27,061 --> 00:26:34,060
Newspapers, libraries and shops of all kinds came to rely on the speed and security of pneumatic delivery.

387
00:26:34,061 --> 00:26:39,060
Edith's, a bridal salon in Fond du Lac, Wisconsin,

388
00:26:39,061 --> 00:26:42,060
installed its pneumatic system in 1949.

389
00:26:42,061 --> 00:26:45,060
It's a very simple machine,

390
00:26:45,061 --> 00:26:47,060
but a great time saver for us here.

391
00:26:47,061 --> 00:26:51,060
We basically ran our complete store with this system.

392
00:26:51,061 --> 00:26:54,060
Upstairs, after a sale,

393
00:26:54,061 --> 00:26:56,060
the sales clerk would take the money,

394
00:26:56,061 --> 00:26:58,060
they would put it in a little cup,

395
00:26:58,061 --> 00:27:00,060
and then they would put it in the chute.

396
00:27:00,061 --> 00:27:00,761
shoot.

397
00:27:00,762 --> 00:27:09,440
Downstairs, the office clerk would take it out of the basket, they would make the change on

398
00:27:09,441 --> 00:27:13,360
it, or do whatever they had to do, look up the balance, and then they would send the cop back

399
00:27:13,361 --> 00:27:19,140
out. A central cashier provided accurate bookkeeping and security for transactions.

400
00:27:20,020 --> 00:27:23,232
This is the blower motor system down here.

401
00:27:23,233 --> 00:27:26,520
The air comes through this pipe right here,

402
00:27:26,521 --> 00:27:31,340
It goes through the blower, and the motor is on the other side of there,

403
00:27:31,560 --> 00:27:33,840
and then it gets exhausted through this pipe right here.

404
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:38,940
There's also a gate valve which controls the amount of airflow that goes through the system.

405
00:27:39,780 --> 00:27:42,280
This is the last working system in the state of Wisconsin.

406
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:48,380
Though retired in 2004, Edith's pneumatic system still stands ready to fill in

407
00:27:48,381 --> 00:27:50,440
should the computers that replaced it ever fail.

408
00:27:50,441 --> 00:27:58,440
Following World War II, electronic cash registers and later fax machines cut deep into the pneumatic

409
00:27:58,441 --> 00:27:59,440
tubes business.

410
00:27:59,441 --> 00:28:06,880
But you'll still find them in facilities like banks, where drive up windows provide convenience

411
00:28:06,881 --> 00:28:08,880
and security for transferring money.

412
00:28:08,881 --> 00:28:15,120
Can you put your driver's license on this tube for me, please?

413
00:28:15,121 --> 00:28:20,400
And the basic technology remains remarkably similar to that first introduced over 150 years

414
00:28:20,401 --> 00:28:24,200
ago.

415
00:28:24,201 --> 00:28:29,260
The vacuum technology used to move money and documents through underground tube systems

416
00:28:29,261 --> 00:28:31,740
was also once used to move people.

417
00:28:31,741 --> 00:28:38,420
We're standing at the site of the first subway in New York, the Beach-Nomatic transit line.

418
00:28:38,421 --> 00:28:42,840
It ran from Broadway and Warren Street, one block down to Murray Street.

419
00:28:42,841 --> 00:28:46,120
It was powered by machinery in the building at Broadway and Warren

420
00:28:46,121 --> 00:28:49,240
that pushed the car down to the end of the tunnel, the one block length,

421
00:28:49,700 --> 00:28:51,220
and then it could pull the car back.

422
00:28:52,620 --> 00:28:58,960
In 1869, inventor Alfred E. Beach began construction on his pneumatic railway

423
00:28:58,961 --> 00:29:01,680
directly across the street from New York City Hall.

424
00:29:02,420 --> 00:29:06,160
To power his train, he had the Roots Company of Connerville, Indiana,

425
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:10,700
build a very large blower, which was dubbed the Western Tornado.

426
00:29:10,701 --> 00:29:18,340
It was one of the largest ever built, but it would power a very small subway, just 300 feet in length.

427
00:29:19,820 --> 00:29:23,900
Like today, in 1869, Broadway was a busy street.

428
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:28,100
The public was initially cautious about going down into a tunnel beneath it.

429
00:29:28,660 --> 00:29:31,600
So to allay fears, Beach made his subway attractive.

430
00:29:32,860 --> 00:29:36,760
Chandeliers hung from the ceiling, and seats were upholstered in fine fabric.

431
00:29:36,761 --> 00:29:44,140
curiosity about Beech's novelty attracted thousands but the thrill of a one-block subway ride soon

432
00:29:44,141 --> 00:29:50,680
faded powerful property owners along Broadway opposed any kind of tunneling beneath them

433
00:29:50,681 --> 00:29:59,200
after three years the pneumatic railway closed the current subway built in 1912

434
00:29:59,201 --> 00:30:06,700
incorporated Beech's tunnel in its path the city hall station is twice the length of Beech's

435
00:30:06,701 --> 00:30:07,754
entire subway.

436
00:30:07,755 --> 00:30:13,400
While a vacuum pressure subway may have failed, smaller pneumatic transport

437
00:30:13,401 --> 00:30:15,020
systems are true successes.

438
00:30:15,021 --> 00:30:20,020
What's changed is how we control it, how we harness that power, particularly in the

439
00:30:20,021 --> 00:30:21,417
use of computer controls.

440
00:30:21,418 --> 00:30:25,160
With computer controls, we can optimize the flow path, we can fully

441
00:30:25,161 --> 00:30:31,600
analyze what the system is doing and better deliver the product.

442
00:30:31,601 --> 00:30:34,400
That sucking sound you hear may soon be a vacuum tube

443
00:30:34,401 --> 00:30:38,000
doing a job for you.

444
00:30:38,001 --> 00:30:40,100
The pull of vacuum suction turns up

445
00:30:40,101 --> 00:30:42,240
in some remarkable places, including

446
00:30:42,241 --> 00:30:44,460
beneath the waves of the Pacific, where

447
00:30:44,461 --> 00:30:47,040
it's battling an unwanted alien invader.

448
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:54,960
Amid coral reefs beneath tropical Hawaiian seas,

449
00:30:54,961 --> 00:30:57,700
marine biologists are using a vacuum as a weapon

450
00:30:57,701 --> 00:31:00,560
against unwanted invaders.

451
00:31:00,561 --> 00:31:02,160
They've dubbed it the super sucker.

452
00:31:03,620 --> 00:31:06,980
The super sucker is basically a giant underwater vacuum cleaner

453
00:31:06,981 --> 00:31:10,020
that we're using to remove alien algal species from reefs in Hawaii.

454
00:31:10,200 --> 00:31:13,540
So alien algal species are species of algae not native to Hawaii

455
00:31:13,541 --> 00:31:16,600
that have been introduced and are damaging the reefs here.

456
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:19,000
When we go down and we're diving,

457
00:31:19,240 --> 00:31:21,660
it's pretty easy to identify the algae that we're looking at.

458
00:31:21,661 --> 00:31:25,240
The reef is maybe 20%, 30% healthy, vibrant coral,

459
00:31:25,380 --> 00:31:27,660
and then about 70% something else entirely.

460
00:31:27,661 --> 00:31:31,740
that something else entirely tends to be just about all alien algae.

461
00:31:34,300 --> 00:31:38,420
Corals are actually tiny marine animals that group together in colonies.

462
00:31:39,640 --> 00:31:43,320
The reefs that they form are composed of millions of their hard skeletons.

463
00:31:45,600 --> 00:31:46,980
Coral needs sunlight to grow.

464
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:51,300
And when overwhelmed by non-native algae that has no local predator,

465
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:53,840
corals die.

466
00:31:54,700 --> 00:31:59,020
So the problem of alien algae overgrowing reefs in Hawaii is a very big problem.

467
00:31:59,100 --> 00:32:00,120
It's a very complex problem.

468
00:32:00,440 --> 00:32:02,960
These complex problems require very comprehensive solutions.

469
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:05,820
Their solution was the super sucker.

470
00:32:06,640 --> 00:32:08,940
So what happens is we get two divers.

471
00:32:09,080 --> 00:32:09,780
They get into the water.

472
00:32:11,420 --> 00:32:13,580
They'll take the suction hose from the barge.

473
00:32:14,320 --> 00:32:17,800
Once they're set, they'll signal the operations coordinator on the barge

474
00:32:17,801 --> 00:32:20,040
to basically turn the pump on and increase suction.

475
00:32:20,041 --> 00:32:24,980
So we'll dive down, we'll go to where the algae is,

476
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:28,840
and we'll basically just hand-feed algae into the suction end of the pump.

477
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:34,580
The super sucker can vacuum over 800 pounds of algae an hour.

478
00:32:35,600 --> 00:32:41,900
It would take 10 divers and 150 volunteers to do the same job using the old hand removal process.

479
00:32:43,220 --> 00:32:47,500
The concept of vacuuming algae from the bottom of the bay is not a difficult one.

480
00:32:47,501 --> 00:32:48,260
That's pretty easy.

481
00:32:48,600 --> 00:32:51,080
But a couple of requirements that we had to adhere to were,

482
00:32:51,400 --> 00:32:52,680
first off, remove the algae.

483
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:56,100
Second off, don't harm the marine environment in the process.

484
00:32:56,460 --> 00:32:58,040
That second part was the real challenge.

485
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:01,280
To satisfy this requirement,

486
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:05,200
the algae is suctioned onto sorting tables on the deck of the super sucker.

487
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:09,400
Once the water and algae lands on the sorting table,

488
00:33:09,800 --> 00:33:11,040
it's the job of our sorters.

489
00:33:11,140 --> 00:33:12,780
We generally have three or four sorters.

490
00:33:12,781 --> 00:33:14,900
Their job is to sort through the algae,

491
00:33:14,901 --> 00:33:19,940
pick out any incidental marine life that may have got sucked up in the process of collecting the algae.

492
00:33:20,160 --> 00:33:23,420
We'll put it in a bucket of water, and at the end of the dive, we'll return it to the reef.

493
00:33:24,320 --> 00:33:28,260
It vacuums over 700 gallons of water a minute onto the sorting table.

494
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:33,740
To create that kind of suction, the super sucker needs an efficient pump.

495
00:33:34,320 --> 00:33:36,160
This is the power behind our operation.

496
00:33:36,300 --> 00:33:39,800
This is a 40-horsepower diesel-powered centrifugal pump.

497
00:33:39,801 --> 00:33:44,920
It's got a five-inch intake, and it works on a Venturi pump configuration.

498
00:33:45,720 --> 00:33:51,420
A Venturi pump forces water through a narrowing passage, where its velocity and pressure increases.

499
00:33:52,420 --> 00:33:55,860
Water then travels into a wider passage, where pressure decreases.

500
00:33:57,620 --> 00:34:02,920
This low pressure draws water from an intake port, expelling it along with the dry water.

501
00:34:02,921 --> 00:34:09,100
And the beauty of the Venturi pump system is that any incidental marine life that comes

502
00:34:09,101 --> 00:34:13,880
up with the algae can be separated from the algae, put in a holding bucket, and though

503
00:34:13,881 --> 00:34:17,300
they've gone for the ride of their life, we can return them back to the reef unharmed.

504
00:34:20,800 --> 00:34:25,680
Once collected, the algae is bagged and given to organic farmers, who use it as fertilizer

505
00:34:25,681 --> 00:34:26,460
for their crops.

506
00:34:26,461 --> 00:34:35,960
Designed as a prototype, the super sucker is actually a modified gold mining dredge,

507
00:34:36,380 --> 00:34:38,900
built by Keen Engineering in Chatsworth, California.

508
00:34:39,900 --> 00:34:46,560
Well, a gold dredge is a machine that basically vacuums the material from the bottom of river streams,

509
00:34:47,140 --> 00:34:49,620
brings it up to the surface of the river,

510
00:34:49,621 --> 00:34:55,360
and generally the material will flow through a long trough,

511
00:34:55,820 --> 00:34:57,260
what we call a sluice box,

512
00:34:57,520 --> 00:35:02,220
and as the material rolls over these series of riffles, traps, and screens,

513
00:35:02,560 --> 00:35:06,400
it separates anything with the heavier specific gravity, such as gold.

514
00:35:07,180 --> 00:35:09,360
When you're operating a dredge for gold mining,

515
00:35:09,680 --> 00:35:12,480
we'll have one guy who's keeping the rocks clear

516
00:35:12,481 --> 00:35:15,180
from the guy who's actually operating the hose or the nozzle

517
00:35:15,181 --> 00:35:16,880
that's sucking up the material.

518
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:22,980
The heavy gold accumulates in the cracks and crevices under those rocks.

519
00:35:24,840 --> 00:35:27,660
This is one of the reasons that we love dredging for gold.

520
00:35:28,360 --> 00:35:32,820
These are some example of some of the different nuggets that I found over dredging over the years.

521
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:35,380
There's no greater high than this.

522
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:38,680
But keen suction finds more underwater than gold.

523
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:46,540
Archaeologists and treasure hunters use keen vacuums to excavate shipwrecks.

524
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:49,260
and other submerged artifacts lost beneath the sea.

525
00:35:50,840 --> 00:35:54,460
What they're doing is vacuuming off the sand and layers

526
00:35:54,461 --> 00:35:58,900
off of the wrecks, exposing a lot of the artifacts.

527
00:36:00,400 --> 00:36:03,460
Underwater explorer Mel Fisher and his salvage company

528
00:36:03,461 --> 00:36:06,120
relied heavily on underwater vacuums

529
00:36:06,121 --> 00:36:08,180
in the excavation of the Henrietta Marie,

530
00:36:08,940 --> 00:36:11,360
a slave ship that sank in 1702,

531
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:14,740
35 miles off the coast of Key West, Florida.

532
00:36:14,741 --> 00:36:20,040
the vacuums helped unearth an irreplaceable piece of a dark side of U.S. history.

533
00:36:21,620 --> 00:36:24,940
The vacuums that salvage wrecks or discover gold

534
00:36:24,941 --> 00:36:28,760
are dwarfed by the titans that keep our waterways navigable.

535
00:36:31,280 --> 00:36:34,200
Millions of tons of sand and silt on river bottoms

536
00:36:34,201 --> 00:36:37,680
must be regularly removed to facilitate marine traffic.

537
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:43,020
The Eagle One is a hopper dredge

538
00:36:43,021 --> 00:36:47,360
that maintains the depth of the lower Mississippi River using powerful suction.

539
00:36:47,900 --> 00:36:52,820
The hopper dredge itself is a floating vacuum cleaner.

540
00:36:53,380 --> 00:36:56,180
It's also been referred to as a self-loading dump truck.

541
00:36:56,740 --> 00:36:59,240
It carries 6,000 cubic yards of material,

542
00:36:59,720 --> 00:37:01,920
and it can load itself in a little over an hour.

543
00:37:02,680 --> 00:37:06,500
It can take that material and dump it in less than 15 minutes.

544
00:37:08,440 --> 00:37:12,480
Global positioning units help the Eagle One determine how much sediment to remove

545
00:37:12,481 --> 00:37:15,020
to maintain the required depth of 51 feet.

546
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:17,040
Right now we're dredging,

547
00:37:17,660 --> 00:37:20,980
and this is the slurry that's coming in

548
00:37:20,981 --> 00:37:22,760
from the drag arms that are over,

549
00:37:23,280 --> 00:37:24,460
that went over on the side.

550
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:30,940
The hopper dredge works by lowering drag arms

551
00:37:30,941 --> 00:37:34,020
with intake openings down to the silt-covered river bottom.

552
00:37:34,660 --> 00:37:37,220
The drag arms hold at 3 miles per hour,

553
00:37:37,760 --> 00:37:40,300
vacuum material up through pipes to the deck of the ship.

554
00:37:40,301 --> 00:37:47,300
After filling its hopper, the dredge moves to a deposit area to dump its load.

555
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:56,180
Despite having pumps that can move 50,000 gallons a minute, a dredger's work on the

556
00:37:56,181 --> 00:37:57,973
Mississippi is never done.

557
00:37:57,974 --> 00:38:02,180
No sooner is one layer of silt removed than another begins to

558
00:38:02,181 --> 00:38:02,881
collect.

559
00:38:02,882 --> 00:38:08,120
The suction power of vacuums prevents traffic on the world's busiest shipping channel

560
00:38:08,121 --> 00:38:12,780
from coming to a standstill.

561
00:38:12,781 --> 00:38:15,320
It also saves America's space program,

562
00:38:15,321 --> 00:38:19,440
a trip outside the ionosphere to test equipment.

563
00:38:19,441 --> 00:38:22,520
NASA uses the world's largest vacuum chamber instead.

564
00:38:27,120 --> 00:38:29,280
When NASA wants to test equipment destined

565
00:38:29,281 --> 00:38:33,180
for the vacuum of space, it has two options.

566
00:38:33,181 --> 00:38:38,040
Go 145 miles straight up, or go to a remote testing

567
00:38:38,041 --> 00:38:43,840
lab outside Sandusky, Ohio, called the Space Power Facility at Glen Plum Brook Station.

568
00:38:44,940 --> 00:38:50,280
The Space Power Facility is the largest space simulation facility in the world, which of

569
00:38:50,281 --> 00:38:54,600
course is made up of the largest thermal vacuum facility. There's nothing even close

570
00:38:54,601 --> 00:38:56,890
to this that was ever built.

571
00:38:56,891 --> 00:39:01,960
It's got a diameter of 100 foot and a height of 122 foot. Main

572
00:39:01,961 --> 00:39:09,560
One vacuum chamber holds 863,000 cubic feet of air, and all that air is pumped out of

573
00:39:09,561 --> 00:39:13,620
the chamber to simulate the conditions of space.

574
00:39:13,621 --> 00:39:17,600
Tests conducted in the vacuum chamber include some of the most important research for America's

575
00:39:17,601 --> 00:39:18,600
space program.

576
00:39:18,601 --> 00:39:23,200
We've done quite a bit of testing for the International Space Station.

577
00:39:23,201 --> 00:39:28,080
We've done a significant amount of testing for the big launch vehicles that have been

578
00:39:28,081 --> 00:39:31,980
developed to launch big payloads into orbit.

579
00:39:31,981 --> 00:39:33,660
Of course, we were heavily involved

580
00:39:33,661 --> 00:39:36,180
with the Mars exploration program,

581
00:39:36,181 --> 00:39:40,520
where the testing of the airbag landing system was done here.

582
00:39:40,521 --> 00:39:44,020
The facility sits on 6,400 acres of land,

583
00:39:44,021 --> 00:39:47,260
buffered by a safe zone due to the potentially high volatility

584
00:39:47,261 --> 00:39:51,580
of experiments conducted here, such as rocket firings.

585
00:39:51,581 --> 00:39:54,400
At the time of its construction in 1969,

586
00:39:54,401 --> 00:39:57,540
the prospect of using nuclear power for space transport

587
00:39:57,541 --> 00:40:01,440
dictated building an enormous chamber within a chamber to contain radiation.

588
00:40:02,580 --> 00:40:05,900
To prevent the chamber from imploding while in vacuum conditions,

589
00:40:06,460 --> 00:40:09,200
the outer chamber's concrete walls are 8 feet thick

590
00:40:09,201 --> 00:40:12,180
and support 140 million pounds of atmosphere.

591
00:40:13,640 --> 00:40:17,280
The giant inner chamber, built from 2,200 tons of aluminum,

592
00:40:17,281 --> 00:40:19,820
is where NASA simulates space.

593
00:40:20,860 --> 00:40:25,740
It takes approximately 8 minutes to close the 50 foot by 50 foot inner door

594
00:40:25,741 --> 00:40:28,380
It weighs 180,000 pounds.

595
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:32,740
Once the 5 million pound outer chamber door has been closed and sealed,

596
00:40:33,060 --> 00:40:36,400
the atmosphere inside can be reduced to a near-perfect vacuum.

597
00:40:37,620 --> 00:40:42,500
It takes us eight hours to pump all the air out of the facility to get the vacuum conditions.

598
00:40:43,720 --> 00:40:49,740
A series of pumps removes an amazing 60 tons of air from the chamber in three stages.

599
00:40:51,700 --> 00:40:54,020
Stage one employs large root pumps.

600
00:40:54,940 --> 00:40:58,220
Positive displacement pumps designed to move lots of air very quickly.

601
00:40:59,760 --> 00:41:04,860
After the root pumps have extracted so many air molecules that they can no longer efficiently move them,

602
00:41:05,560 --> 00:41:07,660
stage 2 rotary vane pumps take over.

603
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:12,680
A rotary vane pump rotates around the interior of a barrel

604
00:41:12,681 --> 00:41:15,760
and scrapes away most of the remaining air molecules.

605
00:41:17,220 --> 00:41:20,360
Phase 3 pumps are found beneath the vacuum chamber.

606
00:41:20,361 --> 00:41:29,860
What we have over here is our high vacuum pumps, and these high vacuum pumps are used to get the last bit of air out of the chamber.

607
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:36,580
They're called cryogenic pumps, and essentially what they do is they freeze the air out with very, very cold surfaces.

608
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:41,300
The cold surfaces are open to the bottom of the chamber floor.

609
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:47,720
One by one, the remaining molecules find their way to these surfaces by bouncing off the chamber walls.

610
00:41:48,420 --> 00:41:52,060
When the molecules hit the cold surfaces, they freeze and stick to them.

611
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:56,560
That's how we pull the last bits of air out of the chamber

612
00:41:56,561 --> 00:42:00,300
to get the hard vacuum conditions that we need to simulate space.

613
00:42:01,560 --> 00:42:05,960
If you're working inside, you might want to bring a spacesuit without one.

614
00:42:06,440 --> 00:42:10,200
First thing that would happen is their lungs would tend to blow out

615
00:42:10,201 --> 00:42:12,300
because of the air that's trapped in their lungs.

616
00:42:12,860 --> 00:42:16,560
Very shortly thereafter, all the fluids in their body would evaporate

617
00:42:16,561 --> 00:42:21,060
because the pressure is so low that the fluids will actually boil away.

618
00:42:21,640 --> 00:42:22,720
So your blood would boil.

619
00:42:23,920 --> 00:42:27,340
Any other fluids in your organs would quickly evaporate and boil.

620
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:29,980
You would, of course, die of asphyxiation

621
00:42:29,981 --> 00:42:32,040
because there's no oxygen in that environment.

622
00:42:33,060 --> 00:42:36,400
Death would be very painful and happen rather quickly.

623
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:41,060
The Space Power Facility's unique capacity to simulate the vacuum

624
00:42:41,061 --> 00:42:43,720
and temperature conditions of space on a large scale

625
00:42:43,721 --> 00:42:48,000
make it the perfect choice to test NASA's next generation of manned craft.

626
00:42:50,380 --> 00:42:54,600
The Orion Crew Exploration Vehicle, which will replace the space shuttle,

627
00:42:55,180 --> 00:42:57,800
will do all of its space simulation testing right here.

628
00:42:58,900 --> 00:43:03,380
It's scheduled for takeoff atop the Ares I launch vehicle in 2014.

629
00:43:05,700 --> 00:43:09,360
Whether in the final frontier or the depths of the ocean,

630
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:12,100
disaster sites or hospitals,

631
00:43:12,101 --> 00:43:15,100
Vacuums are ready to suck it up.

632
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:19,200
They can draw, lift, haul, or transport.

633
00:43:19,300 --> 00:43:22,140
And they're pretty handy for tidying up around the house.

634
00:43:22,240 --> 00:43:24,240
I don't know what it is I like about them.

635
00:43:24,340 --> 00:43:25,940
I just love this thing.

636
00:43:26,040 --> 00:43:27,940
And apparently, so do we.

